  "summary": "An architectural style reviving elements of Islamic architecture from North Africa and Spain.",
  "description": "Characterized by horseshoe arches, domes, and intricate geometric patterns, it emphasized exoticism and romanticism. This style emerged during European colonial expansion, particularly in Spain and regions with Islamic heritage, as a nostalgic romanticization of Moorish rule.",
  "culturalContext": "Moorish Revival — 19th-century Europe and America rebuilding the Alhambra — produced synagogues (Berlin's Oranienburger Straße, Budapest's Dohány, Cincinnati's Plum Street), railway stations, casinos and pleasure gardens in horseshoe arches, honeycomb vaults, azulejo fields and minaret towers. For Jewish congregations the style evoked Golden-Age Sephardic Spain; for entertainers it packaged exotic leisure. The style's most photographed survivors (Granada's cafés, Sarajevo's Vijećnica) tie Victorian exoticism to 21st-century memory politics.",
